 "drivingQuestion": "In what locale do R' Yehuda and the Rabbis dispute the yoke sale?",
 "outcome": "inconclusive",
 "outcomeNote": "question answered in the next unit (לא צריכא)",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "bava_batra.77b.7",
   "daf": "77b",
   "sentenceId": 6,
   "he": "מָכַר אֶת הַצֶּמֶד – לֹא מָכַר אֶת הַבָּקָר וְכוּ׳. הֵיכִי דָמֵי? אִילֵּימָא דְּקָרוּ לְצִימְדָּא ״צִימְדָּא״ וּלְבָקָר ״בָּקָר״ – פְּשִׁיטָא; צִימְדָּא זַבֵּין לֵיהּ, בָּקָר לָא זַבֵּין לֵיהּ! וְאֶלָּא דְּקָרוּ לֵיהּ נָמֵי לְבָקָר ״צִימְדָּא״? כּוּלֵּיהּ זַבֵּין לֵיהּ!",
   "en": "The mishna teaches: One who «sold a yoke has not sold the oxen;» and the Rabbis and Rabbi Yehuda disagree over whether the sum of money proves exactly what was sold. The Gemara analyzes their disagreement: «What are the circumstances? If we say» that the mishna is referring to a place «where they call a yoke: Tzimda, and» they call «oxen: Bakar,» it is «obvious» that «he sold him a yoke» and «did not sell him» the «oxen. But» if the mishna is referring to a place «where they also call oxen: Tzimda,» then the seller «sold him everything.»"
  }
 ],
